E=[
(1,1871,"Sky","A red body over Marseilles","Coggia at Marseilles reports a large red body moving slowly across the sky, visible for 15 minutes. Guillemin and de Fonville reported similar sightings that year. Fort cites the Chemical News.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Chem. News, 24-193","large body, colored red, moving slowly, visible 15 minutes, reported by Coggia, Marseilles, Aug. 1, 1871"),
(2,1865,"Falls","A stone wedge falls near Cashel","A stone shaped like a wedge falls from the sky near Cashel, Tipperary. The Proceedings of the Royal Irish Academy did not question the fall, but called it 'pyramidal' rather than ax-like.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Proc. Roy. Irish Acad., 9-337","a stone wedge that fell from the sky, near Cashel, Tipperary, Aug. 2, 1865"),
(5,1889,"Sky","A slow-falling object that exploded at Twickenham","During a violent storm an object about 15 inches long and 5 inches wide falls rather slowly at East Twickenham. It explodes and no substance is found. Fort cites Nature.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 40-415","Aug. 5, 1889, during a violent storm, an object that looked to be about 15 inches long and 5 inches wide, fell, rather slowly, at East Twickenham"),
(6,1867,"Cosmos","A rising oval spot on Linne","Buckingham sees a 'rising oval spot' on Linne while the crater is in darkness. In October the crater appears as a convex white spot. Fort cites the British Association report.","NL","New Lands, citing Rept. B. A., 1867-7","Buckingham saw upon Linné, which was in darkness"),
(7,1804,"Mysteries","A dark mist over Grenada","The heavens are obscured by a dark mist at Grenada. Fort lists it in Mallet's catalogue of earthquakes accompanied by strange appearances in the sky.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Mallet's catalogue","Grenada, Aug. 7, 1804"),
(8,1849,"Sky","Thousands of white objects over Gais","At 3 p.m. at Gais, Switzerland, Sir Robert Inglis sees thousands and thousands of brilliant white objects like snowflakes in a cloudless sky for about twenty-five minutes. Fort cites his letter to Col. Sabine.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Rept. Brit. Assoc., 1849-17","at 3 P.M., Aug. 8, 1849, at Gais, Switzerland, Inglis had seen thousands and thousands of brilliant white objects"),
(9,1892,"Falls","Mussels fall on Paderborn","A yellow cloud appears over Paderborn, Germany, and a torrential rain falls containing hundreds of mussels. Fort cites Nature, quoting Das Wetter.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 47-278 and Das Wetter, Dec. 1892","upon Aug. 9, 1892, a yellow cloud appeared over Paderborn, Germany"),
(10,1897,"Falls","Broken sheet ice at Manassas","Rough-edged pieces of ice with smooth surfaces, about two inches across and one inch thick, fall at Manassas, Virginia. Fort says they look like fragments of a smooth sheet of ice.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Symons' Met. Mag., 32-172","that fell at Manassas, Virginia, Aug. 10, 1897"),
(11,1805,"Mysteries","Explosions at East Haddam","An explosive sound is heard at East Haddam, Connecticut. Fort records six earlier such sounds there since 1791, and notes the Moodus-like 'guns' that kept coming for years.","NL","New Lands, citing Amer. Jour. Sci., 39-339","Upon August 11, 1805, an explosive sound was heard at East Haddam, Connecticut"),
(12,1910,"Sea","A horizontal wheel of flashes in the South China Sea","At midnight Capt. Breyer of the steamer Valentijn sees a rotation of flashes like a horizontal wheel turning rapidly. The Danish Meteorological Institute noted that this time the wheel was above the water.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Danish Meteorological Institute","midnight, Aug. 12, 1910, he saw a rotation of flashes"),
(13,1819,"Falls","The Amherst light","On the evening of Aug. 13 a light is seen at Amherst, Massachusetts, a falling object with a sound like an explosion. It lights a wall in Prof. Dewey's house. Prof. Hitchcock later went to see the object.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Amer. Jour. Sci., 1-2-335","upon the evening of August 13, 1819, a light was seen in Amherst"),
(14,1888,"Falls","A shower of ink at the Cape","At the Cape of Good Hope a rain so black falls that it is described as a 'shower of ink'. Fort uses it to show black rains occur far from manufacturing centres.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing La Nature, 1888, 2-406","Aug. 14, 1888, there fell at the Cape of Good Hope"),
(17,1887,"Falls","The iron spheroid of Brixton","A small lump of iron two inches in diameter is said to have fallen during a thunderstorm at Brixton. Mr. Symons says 'At present I cannot trace it.' Fort later describes it as an oblate spheroid.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing London Times, Feb. 1, 1888","fallen, during a thunderstorm, at Brixton, Aug. 17, 1887"),
(18,1912,"Mysteries","Another concussion at Holbrook","A month after the great stone fall of July 19, there is another concussion at Holbrook, Arizona. The Seismological Society listed it as an earthquake. Fort asks what else it might have been.","NL","New Lands, citing Bull. Seis. Soc. Amer., 1-209","About a month later (Aug. 18) there was another concussion at Holbrook"),
(19,1887,"Sky","The thing seen over Marseilles","Fort opens New Lands with a sky object seen over the city of Marseilles, which he says you may ride 'bearing down upon the moon'. It is one of the data of his first chapter.","NL","New Lands, chapter one","the thing that was seen over the city of Marseilles, Aug. 19, 1887"),
(20,1870,"Falls","Salt crystals in a Swiss hailstorm","Large crystals of salt fall in a hailstorm in Switzerland. The orthodox explanation, that the salt came from the Mediterranean, is called a 'crime' by Fort. He cites Annuaire de Rec. Sci.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing An. Rec. Sci., 1872","Large crystals of salt fell--in a hailstorm--Aug. 20, 1870, in Switzerland"),
(24,1918,"Falls","Sand eels rain on Hindon","During heavy rain and thunder, hundreds of small fishes identified as sand eels fall at Hindon, a suburb of Sunderland, in an area of about 60 by 30 yards. A correspondent from the Dove Marine Laboratory told Nature.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, Sept. 19, 1918, p. 46","at Hindon, a suburb of Sunderland, Aug. 24, 1918, hundreds of small fishes"),
(26,1894,"Sky","The flatfish in the sky over North Wales","A 'most extraordinary and singular phenomenon' is seen over North Wales: a disk from which projected an orange body looking like an elongated flatfish. Admiral Ommanney reported it to Nature.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 50-524","North Wales, Aug. 26, 1894"),
(27,1885,"Sky","A strange object at Bermuda","At about 8:30 a.m., Mrs. Adelina D. Bassett sees 'a strange object in the clouds, coming from the north' at Bermuda and calls Mrs. L. Lowell to see it. The Royal Gazette of Bermuda carried the account, and General Lefroy sent it to the Times.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Royal Gazette of Bermuda, Sept. 8, 1885, and London Times, Sept. 29, 1885","upon Aug. 27, 1885, at about 8:30 A.M., there was observed by Mrs. Adelina D. Bassett"),
(28,1883,"Sky","Capt. Noble's glorious comet","At 10:35 p.m. Capt. Noble sees in the sky something 'like a new and most glorious comet', with a tail like a searchlight. He sketched it for Knowledge. Fort grants him membership in his 'extra-geographic fold'.","NL","New Lands, citing Knowledge, 4-173","night of August 28, 1883, he saw in the sky something"),
(29,1871,"Sky","Trouvelot's marching objects","M. Trouvelot sees objects, some round, some triangular and some of complex forms, moving in parallel lines. Fort says that they seemed not to be moving in the wind. L'Annee Scientifique reported it.","NL","New Lands, citing L'Annee Scientifique, 29-8","observations by M. Trouvelot, Aug. 29, 1871"),
(30,1882,"Falls","Eight-inch ice at Davenport","'Pieces of ice' eight inches long and an inch and a half thick fall at Davenport, Iowa. Fort lists them with other large ice falls in the Monthly Weather Review.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, Aug. 1882","Davenport, Iowa, Aug. 30, 1882"),
(31,1895,"Sky","Dr. Murray's luminous object","A few minutes before 8 p.m. Dr. J. A. H. Murray (later editor of the Oxford English Dictionary) sees at Oxford a luminous object, larger than Venus, sail slowly east as if driven by a strong wind. He wrote to the Times.","NL","New Lands, citing London Times, Sept. 4, 1895","a few minutes before 8 P. M., August 31, 1895, he saw in the sky a luminous object"),
]
